## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical on-device NLP with privacy, multilingual support, and simple explanations—core concerns for real-world edge deployments.

## Key Concepts
- On-device inference and privacy preservation
- Multilingual model distillation and language detection
- Pipeline: classify then extract slots
- Lightweight explainability for model decisions
- Offline evaluation with latency targets

## Code Example
```javascript
function onDeviceInference(text, model) {
  const lang = detectLanguage(text);
  const category = classifier(lang).predict(text);
  const slots = slotTagger(text);
  const explanation = explain(text, category, slots);
  return { category, slots, explanation };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to handle language-detection errors gracefully?
- How would you measure privacy impact and data leakage risk?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates practical on-device ML expertise, cross-language support, privacy-preserving inference, and the ability to balance model size with functionality in a realistic chat application.

## Key Concepts
- On-device machine learning and privacy-first design
- Lightweight multilingual models for resource-constrained environments
- Explainability through keyword attribution and contribution scoring
- Offline labeling pipelines and evaluation methodologies

## Code Example
```javascript
function predict(text) {
  const keywords = extractKeywords(text);
  const embeddings = model.embed(keywords);
  const intent = classifier.predict(embeddings);
  const tone = sentimentAnalyzer.predict(embeddings);
  const explanations = explainer.attribute(keywords, intent);
  return { intent, tone, explanations };
}
```

This design meets all constraints: under 15 MB, on-device execution, multilingual support (EN/ES/PT), explainable predictions, and strong privacy controls.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical, multilingual, on-device NLP component with concrete constraints and a simple evaluation path.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight NER: lexicon + rules, tiny ML fallback
- Multilingual tokenization: EN/ES/DE handling
- Taxonomy normalization and confidence scoring
- On-device privacy and rollout strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
function extractMentions(text, lexicon) {
  const mentions = [];
  for (const [word, label] of Object.entries(lexicon)) {
    if (text.includes(word)) mentions.push({ word, label });
  }
  return mentions;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle ambiguous mentions across languages?
- What metrics and thresholds would you use for the confidence score, and how would you validate them on-device?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design real-time, privacy-preserving, multilingual NLP pipelines with regulatory constraints and governance.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time streaming architectures (Kafka, Kinesis)
- Privacy-preserving embeddings and federated updates
- NER + relation extraction for ADE
- Per-language drift detection and auditability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo drift detector snippet
function klDivergence(p, q) {
  let sum=0;
  for (let i=0;i<p.length;i++){
    if (p[i]===0||q[i]===0) continue;
    sum += p[i]*Math.log(p[i]/q[i]);
  }
  return sum;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle multilingual tokenization for languages with scarce resources?
- What testing strategy ensures regulatory audit trails are verifiable?

